In this lesson, we'll look at the factor theorem, a really handy shortcut for breaking polynomials into pieces. Here's the big idea. If you plug a value into your polynomial and you get zero, then that value is called a root, and it tells you something important: the bracket formed by x minus that value is a factor of the polynomial. The rule works both ways too. If x minus a is a factor, then plugging in a gives zero. So zeros and factors are just two sides of the same coin.

Now, how do you actually use it? Start by trying easy whole number values for x. A handy tip is that any integer root has to divide the constant term, so that narrows down your guesses fast. When you find a value that makes the polynomial equal to zero, you pull out that bracket as a factor, then divide to get a simpler quotient polynomial. Then you repeat the same process on that smaller piece until you've factored everything you can.

This trick is great for solving polynomial equations, because every factor hands you a root on a plate. It also helps you sketch graphs, since every x-intercept matches up with a linear factor. Now let's see it in action.